    \;
  </hide-preamble>

  <with|font-series|bold|Derivations-on-k-algebras> 2026-07-25

  The definition of <with|font-series|bold|derivations> on a commutative <math|k>-algebra <math|A> is the generalization from the concept of derivations on the linear space <math|\<cal*-C\><around*|(|M|)>> consisting of the smooth functions on the manifold <math|M>, which are equivalently the smooth vector fields on <math|M>, denoted as <math|\<frak-X\><around*|(|M|)>=\<Gamma\><around*|(|\<Tau\>M|)>>.

  <with|font-series|bold|Definition> Given a commutative <math|k>-algebra <math|A> where <math|k> is a field (or a commutative ring), we define a subset of <math|End<rsub|k><around*|(|A|)>>,

  <\equation*>
    Deri<rsub|k><around*|(|A|)>\<triangleq\><around*|{|f\<in\>End<rsub|k><around*|(|A|)>\|\<forall\>x,y\<in\>A,f<around*|(|x y|)>=f<around*|(|x|)> y+x f<around*|(|y|)>|}>
  </equation*>

  called the space of derivations on <math|A>. The property <math|f<around*|(|x y|)>=f<around*|(|x|)> y+x f<around*|(|y|)>> is called the <with|font-series|bold|Leibniz law>. <math|Deri<rsub|k><around*|(|A|)>> is itself a <math|k>-linear space (or a <math|k>-module), since the following formulas

  <\align*>
    <tformat|<cwith|1|-1|1|-1|cell-hyphen|t>|<twith|table-hyphen|y>|<table|<row|<cell|<around*|(|f+g|)><around*|(|x|)>>|<cell|\<triangleq\>f<around*|(|x|)>+g<around*|(|x|)>>>|<row|<cell|<around*|(|a f|)><around*|(|x|)>>|<cell|\<triangleq\>a f<around*|(|x|)>>>>>
  </align*>

  define the addition and the scalar multiplication of <math|Deri<rsub|k>>. It is direct to check that both <math|f+g> and <math|a f> are still in the set <math|Deri<rsub|k>>, which ensures the closedness of linear operations on <math|Deri<rsub|k>>.

  <math|\<blacksquare\>>

  However, <math|Deri<rsub|k><around*|(|A|)>> is not itself a <math|k>-algebra, regarding the fact that compositing 2 derivations <math|f> and <math|g> on <math|A> does not result in another new derivation <math|f g\<in\>End<rsub|k><around*|(|A|)>\<supsetneqq\>Deri<rsub|k><around*|(|A|)>> in general. To see this, consider the example that <math|A=\<cal*-C\><rsub|p><around*|(|M|)>> for some <math|p\<in\>M> and <math|f=<frac|\<partial\>|\<partial\>x<rsup|i>>,g=<frac|\<partial\>|\<partial\>x<rsup|j>>>, with <math|i\<neq\>j>. The composition of <math|f> and <math|g> is <math|f g=<frac|\<partial\><rsup|2>|\<partial\>x<rsup|i> \<partial\>x<rsup|j>>>, which is not a derivation, since it does not satisfy that <math|<frac|\<partial\><rsup|2>|\<partial\>x<rsup|i> \<partial\>x<rsup|j>><around*|(|\<varphi\> \<psi\>|)>=<frac|\<partial\><rsup|2>|\<partial\>x<rsup|i> \<partial\>x<rsup|j>><around*|(|\<varphi\>|)> \<psi\>+\<varphi\> <frac|\<partial\><rsup|2>|\<partial\>x<rsup|i> \<partial\>x<rsup|j>><around*|(|\<psi\>|)>>. So composition of 2 derivations is usually not a derivation.

  But we can define such an algebraic operation on <math|Deri<rsub|k><around*|(|A|)>>, called the <with|font-series|bold|commutator>.

  <with|font-series|bold|Theorem> For any pair of derivations <math|f,g\<in\>Deri<rsub|k><around*|(|A|)>>, the commutator of <math|f> and <math|g>

  <\equation*>
    <around*|[|f,g|]>\<triangleq\>f g-g f
  </equation*>

  is a derivation. Since <math|End<rsub|k><around*|(|A|)>> is a <math|k>-algebra, it is closed under multiplication and subtraction. So <math|f g-g f\<in\>End<rsub|k><around*|(|A|)>>. The theorem says that, the commutator <math|<around*|[|f,g|]>> of <math|f> and <math|g> is also in <math|Deri<rsub|k><around*|(|A|)>\<subseteq\>End<rsub|k><around*|(|A|)>>.

  <with|font-series|bold|Proof>

  To prove that <math|h=f g-g f\<in\>Deri<rsub|k><around*|(|A|)>>, we only need to check the Leibniz's law. To check this, we consider

  <\align*>
    <tformat|<cwith|1|-1|1|-1|cell-hyphen|t>|<twith|table-hyphen|y>|<table|<row|<cell|h<around*|(|x y|)>=>|<cell|<around*|(|f g-g f|)><around*|(|x y|)>>>|<row|<cell|=>|<cell|<around*|(|f g|)><around*|(|x y|)>-<around*|(|g f|)><around*|(|x y|)>>>|<row|<cell|=>|<cell|f<around*|(|g<around*|(|x y|)>|)>-g<around*|(|f<around*|(|x y|)>|)>>>|<row|<cell|=>|<cell|f<around*|(|g<around*|(|x|)>y+x g<around*|(|y|)>|)>-g<around*|(|f<around*|(|x|)> y+x f<around*|(|y|)>|)>>>|<row|<cell|=>|<cell|f<around*|(|g<around*|(|x|)> y|)>+f<around*|(|x g<around*|(|y|)>|)>-g<around*|(|f<around*|(|x|)> y|)>-g<around*|(|x f<around*|(|y|)>|)>>>|<row|<cell|=>|<cell|f<around*|(|g<around*|(|x|)>|)> y+g<around*|(|x|)> f<around*|(|y|)>+f<around*|(|x|)> g<around*|(|y|)>+x f<around*|(|g<around*|(|y|)>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|-g<around*|(|f<around*|(|x|)>|)> y-f<around*|(|x|)> g<around*|(|y|)>-g<around*|(|x|)> f<around*|(|y|)>-x g<around*|(|f<around*|(|y|)>|)>>>|<row|<cell|=>|<cell|<around*|(|f g-g f|)><around*|(|x|)> y+x <around*|(|f g-g f|)><around*|(|y|)>>>|<row|<cell|=>|<cell|h<around*|(|x|)> y+x h<around*|(|y|)>>>>>
  </align*>

  Therefore, <math|h> satisfies the Leibniz law, and thus <math|h\<in\>Deri<rsub|k><around*|(|A|)>>.

  <math|\<box\>>

  Note that in the formulas above, the terms <math|f<around*|(|x|)> g<around*|(|y|)>> and <math|g<around*|(|x|)> f<around*|(|y|)>> are both cancelled. Furthermore, these terms are related to the <with|font-series|bold|Poisson bracket> of <math|x> and <math|y>.

  <with|font-series|bold|Definition> Given 2 derivations <math|f> and <math|g> on the <math|k>-algebra <math|A>, the <with|font-series|bold|Poisson bracket> of 2 elements <math|x,y\<in\>A> with respect to <math|f,g> is defined as

  <\equation*>
    <around*|{|x,y|}><rsub|f,g>=f<around*|(|x|)>g<around*|(|y|)>-g<around*|(|x|)>f<around*|(|y|)>
  </equation*>

  <math|\<blacksquare\>>

  The Poisson brackets are closely related to the commutators. In canonical quantization in quantum mechanics, the Poisson bracket of the classical quantities are replaced by the commutator where quantities are changed to operators.

  Thus we have a different algebraic operator on <math|Deri<rsub|k><around*|(|A|)>>, where <math|End<rsub|k><around*|(|A|)>> is the algebra with operator being the composition of linear mappings, and <math|Deri<rsub|k><around*|(|A|)>> is the algebra with operator being the commutator of derivations.

  This space of derivations <math|Deri<rsub|k><around*|(|A|)>>, together with the commutator, is an example of a <with|font-series|bold|Lie algebra>.
</body>

